        {
          "attributes": {
            "author": "Michael Madden",
            "date": "2014-03-27",
            "refID": "REF_02",
            "title": "Use of MathML quotient to compute mass rate in two stage rocket propulsion model"
          },
          "children": [
            {
              "attributes": {},
              "children": [],
              "namespace": "http://daveml.org/2010/DAVEML",
              "tag": "description",
              "text": "An email from Michael Madden caught a syntax error: \"I ran into a small obstacle today trying to run the DAVE-ML model of the two stage rocket. The propulsion model (twostage_prop.dml) uses the MathML token 'quotient' to compute the mass rate. Our DAVE-ML translator does not handle the quotient token (though it is not too difficult to add). Nevertheless, I was wondering if there was a specific reason to use quotient over divide. The former would produce mass rates that are truncated whole numbers of kg/s; the latter would, of course, allow mass rates with decimal fractions of kg/s.\""
            }
          ],
          "namespace": "http://daveml.org/2010/DAVEML",
          "tag": "reference",
          "text": null
        },
        {
          "attributes": {
            "date": "2014-03-27",
            "modID": "A",
            "refID": "REF_02"
          },
          "children": [
            {
              "attributes": {
